WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:02.340
Instructor: All right, so now we're onto a new command,

00:02.340 --> 00:03.630
git restore,

00:03.630 --> 00:06.540
and this is actually a new command to git.

00:06.540 --> 00:09.570
It's pretty new, it's not all that well-known,

00:09.570 --> 00:12.660
so there's a lot of existing tutorials and videos

00:12.660 --> 00:15.840
and courses that don't mention it at all.

00:15.840 --> 00:19.350
Git restore takes some of the burden away from git checkout.

00:19.350 --> 00:21.630
It does- remember how I've said this a couple times,

00:21.630 --> 00:23.490
git checkout does a lot of things,

00:23.490 --> 00:26.400
and git switch alongside git restore,

00:26.400 --> 00:29.280
they both were introduced to try and take away

00:29.280 --> 00:31.350
some of that stuff from git checkout.

00:31.350 --> 00:33.240
So, git checkout still does all that stuff,

00:33.240 --> 00:36.060
but now, we have standalone commands git switch,

00:36.060 --> 00:37.020
to switch branches,

00:37.020 --> 00:40.530
and git restore, that helps with undoing operations.

00:40.530 --> 00:41.610
As you've seen already,

00:41.610 --> 00:43.230
we can use checkout to switch branches,

00:43.230 --> 00:46.470
we can use checkout to checkout earlier commits.

00:46.470 --> 00:49.320
We can use checkout, as we just saw in the previous video,

00:49.320 --> 00:51.690
to undo or discard changes.

00:51.690 --> 00:56.280
So, it's kind of crazy, but restore is the new command.

00:56.280 --> 00:58.983
It sometimes, honestly, feels a bit clunky to me,

01:00.060 --> 01:01.800
but I'm gonna show it to you now.

01:01.800 --> 01:03.480
So, the first thing we can do with git restore

01:03.480 --> 01:07.590
is discard changes that we've made in a repository

01:07.590 --> 01:08.940
since the last commit.

01:08.940 --> 01:11.550
Basically, the same thing I showed you in the last video.

01:11.550 --> 01:15.330
So, if I make some changes in my file, let's say dog,

01:15.330 --> 01:17.820
I add a bunch of stuff again.

01:17.820 --> 01:20.010
I delete all of this, right?

01:20.010 --> 01:22.980
I write, like, whatever that is, "-DOGGGG!!"

01:22.980 --> 01:25.290
Okay, I make a bunch of changes.

01:25.290 --> 01:26.670
I haven't added or committed them.

01:26.670 --> 01:28.140
I type git status, right?

01:28.140 --> 01:31.080
I've modified dog and I realize, "Ah, you know what?

01:31.080 --> 01:32.430
I don't want any of that."

01:32.430 --> 01:36.300
I can type git restore and then dog.txt.

01:36.300 --> 01:39.210
I don't even have to reference head or anything like that.

01:39.210 --> 01:40.920
Just git restore and a file name

01:40.920 --> 01:44.670
and it is going to restore my file, dog,

01:44.670 --> 01:47.130
back to what it looked like at head,

01:47.130 --> 01:50.070
where head currently is, at the last commit that we've made.

01:50.070 --> 01:50.903
So, here we go.

01:52.920 --> 01:56.310
And, there we are, dog.txt back to where it was.

01:56.310 --> 01:59.340
So, this is the same as what we did earlier,

01:59.340 --> 02:03.393
git checkout head dog.txt, it's just a little bit simpler.

02:04.920 --> 02:06.390
And then, the other thing we can do

02:06.390 --> 02:11.390
is actually reference a particular commit and go back.

02:11.550 --> 02:14.160
So, I could restore the contents of my dog file

02:14.160 --> 02:17.850
or my cat file or whatever I want to what it looked like

02:17.850 --> 02:19.200
two commits ago.

02:19.200 --> 02:20.790
So, I'll show you that.

02:20.790 --> 02:22.980
It's a little- this is why I said it's kind of clunky.

02:22.980 --> 02:26.100
We have to use the --source option.

02:26.100 --> 02:28.770
So, git restore and then we specify a source.

02:28.770 --> 02:31.860
The default source is just head, as we saw,

02:31.860 --> 02:34.950
but we can specify a different source using --source

02:34.950 --> 02:38.460
and then, either a commit hash or this-

02:38.460 --> 02:40.200
what do you call this syntax?

02:40.200 --> 02:43.830
Head~1 or head~2 and so on.

02:43.830 --> 02:46.230
So, let's try this out.

02:46.230 --> 02:48.030
It doesn't matter if I have stuff in here or not,

02:48.030 --> 02:50.370
but let's say I screw it up a lot

02:50.370 --> 02:55.350
and I want to restore the dog file back to-

02:55.350 --> 02:58.770
I don't know, if this is head~1,

02:58.770 --> 03:00.060
let's go all the way back to here.

03:00.060 --> 03:02.700
I want to restore it to this commit.

03:02.700 --> 03:06.120
So, this would be head~2.

03:06.120 --> 03:09.630
This is head. Back one, back two.

03:09.630 --> 03:14.393
So, I can do git restore --source head~2

03:18.270 --> 03:20.220
and then the file name.

03:20.220 --> 03:23.700
And, whatever is in here, I have unstaged changes,

03:23.700 --> 03:25.050
I'm going to lose them,

03:25.050 --> 03:27.780
which I don't think I made a big enough deal about earlier.

03:27.780 --> 03:29.850
But, you know, those changes are gone.

03:29.850 --> 03:32.520
If I haven't added or committed those changes,

03:32.520 --> 03:35.190
if I lose those changes, git has no record of them.

03:35.190 --> 03:39.090
So, only use this if you are comfortable truly going back

03:39.090 --> 03:42.000
and getting rid of uncommitted changes that you have

03:42.000 --> 03:43.020
at the moment.

03:43.020 --> 03:44.670
All right? So, here we go.

03:44.670 --> 03:47.730
Restore the contents of dog.txt to what it looked like

03:47.730 --> 03:50.193
two commits prior to head.

03:52.260 --> 03:55.080
And, there we are. First commit.

03:55.080 --> 03:56.700
Now, that doesn't move us, right?

03:56.700 --> 03:59.580
If we type git status, we're still on master.

03:59.580 --> 04:03.750
If I type git log, we're still here on the last commit,

04:03.750 --> 04:05.010
the tip of the master branch.

04:05.010 --> 04:06.360
So, we haven't gone back in time,

04:06.360 --> 04:08.430
we haven't, like, you know, time-traveled,

04:08.430 --> 04:10.140
no detached head or anything.

04:10.140 --> 04:12.750
But, dog.txt has been modified.

04:12.750 --> 04:15.780
So, if I don't want that, if I want to go back to head

04:15.780 --> 04:20.680
on that file, I can still do git restore dog.txt

04:22.140 --> 04:24.630
and now, it goes back to the most recent commit.

04:24.630 --> 04:25.860
So, we still lost-

04:25.860 --> 04:27.570
you know, if we have a bunch of other stuff,

04:27.570 --> 04:30.240
that's gonna be gone unless I add and commit that,

04:30.240 --> 04:32.610
but I can restore back-

04:32.610 --> 04:34.800
one more time, let's go git restore

04:34.800 --> 04:36.840
and then, I'll do it to both files.

04:36.840 --> 04:41.083
So, --source, head, and then I believe it's ~3.

04:42.330 --> 04:44.373
And then, I'll do cat and dog.

04:45.510 --> 04:49.140
Op, nope, I guess there is no head~3, it is-

04:49.140 --> 04:50.910
oh yeah, it's head~2.

04:50.910 --> 04:53.430
Well, here we are, first commit.

04:53.430 --> 04:54.900
First commit on both of them.

04:54.900 --> 04:57.780
And then, if I really didn't want to do that,

04:57.780 --> 05:00.630
or I want to- let's say I want to take cat back

05:00.630 --> 05:02.130
to where head is right now.

05:02.130 --> 05:04.330
Git restore cat

05:06.120 --> 05:09.090
and there, cat goes back to where head is.

05:09.090 --> 05:12.390
Dog is still back two commits prior.

05:12.390 --> 05:14.640
Okay, so, again, this is a very silly example,

05:14.640 --> 05:16.920
but I wanted to do something obvious where we knew

05:16.920 --> 05:18.900
this came from the first commit, this came from second,

05:18.900 --> 05:20.940
this came from third, and this-

05:20.940 --> 05:23.520
you know, having two files makes it easier to demonstrate.

05:23.520 --> 05:24.420
So, there we are.

05:24.420 --> 05:28.200
That is using git restore. At least, one use of it.

05:28.200 --> 05:31.590
In the next video, I'll show you another use of git restore,

05:31.590 --> 05:33.780
but, so far, what we've seen is that,

05:33.780 --> 05:35.820
basically, this is an another way of doing

05:35.820 --> 05:38.370
what we've already seen how to do with git checkout.

05:38.370 --> 05:41.160
Where with git checkout we have to specify head

05:41.160 --> 05:42.240
and then a file name,

05:42.240 --> 05:44.220
and checkout does a million other things,

05:44.220 --> 05:45.570
so it's confusing,

05:45.570 --> 05:48.810
restore is a lot more narrowly focused.

05:48.810 --> 05:51.030
It does, really, well, I guess two things,

05:51.030 --> 05:52.773
but, so far, we've seen one.

05:53.610 --> 05:56.400
And, by default, it will restore based upon head,

05:56.400 --> 05:57.660
we don't have to provide that.

05:57.660 --> 06:01.140
Or, we can provide a source with --source

06:01.140 --> 06:05.283
and then specify a commit hash or use this syntax. Okay.
